00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.929 align:start position:0%
European Union environment ministers  will discuss this week new rules to  monitor report on and check carbon  dioxide emissions from maritime  transport the ministers will weigh  whether the proposals to harmonize  surveillance of a key gas responsible  for global warming are balanced and  flexible enough and do not impose a  heavy administrative burden co2  emissions represent about ninety-eight  percent of the greenhouse gases emitted  by ships it's hoped that the EU model  will serve as an example for the  creation of a global system the european  parliament is scheduled to vote on the  scheme in april just before its  legislative term expires the ministers  will also debate ways to stop the spread  of introduced species into the EU which  are causing severe damage to the  environment invasive alien species are  transported outside their normal habitat  through human action this can hurt the  new ecosystem and have serious economic  and social consequences around 12,000  alien species are thought to be found in  Europe around ten to fifteen percent of  them have spread and a costing the EU at  least 12 billion euros each year the new  measures would involve a prevention plan  an early warning and rapid response  system plus steps to manage the species  as they spread
 

